WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.880
Hi, everyone, and I'm excited
to be here to show you

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:16.149
a connection live between Google
BigQuery and Tableau Online

00:00:16.149 --> 00:00:18.280
right here on the web.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.610
We're in Tableau
Online right now,

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:24.400
and we're looking at several
dashboards that are connected

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.610
live to Google BigQuery.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.910
Let's click into one.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:33.515
What we have here is a
set of data from Twitter

00:00:33.515 --> 00:00:36.140
that we've been collecting with
a number of different hashtags,

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.240
including BigData
and other hashtags.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.360
It's collected by Gnip
Twitter's analytics engine.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.000
And we did select
on a big data set,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.520
and we've got all this data
right here in the dashboard

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:55.160
that we can look at live with
Tableau Online and BigQuery.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.980
Now, what I'm going to do is
I'm going to go ahead and filter

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:01.060
down to the last--
actually, let's go down

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:02.720
to the last day or so of data.

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:13.470
So now we're looking at the last
day of data in this data set,

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.310
and we've been looking
for tweets with text IOT.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.245
But I'm more interested in
tweets with text Google.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:25.870
And so now what we've got
is we've got dashboard

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:28.680
about when people have
been tweeting in this data

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.094
set, which is all the tweets,
as well as about Google,

00:01:32.094 --> 00:01:33.260
which are the yellow tweets.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.020
We've got several views here.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.910
This one tells us that Google
has been tweeted in about 5%

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:41.690
of the data set
here about big data,

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:45.160
and we can see a scatter plot
of all the tweets and all

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.580
the users who are
using this term.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:53.140
Now I'm going to just
click in to the ones that

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:54.010
are using Google.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:08.139
And now you can see all of
these tweets that have been

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:09.949
posted in the last day or so.

00:02:09.949 --> 00:02:14.187
We can hover over one and
look at what the tweet was.

00:02:14.187 --> 00:02:16.270
In this case, they're
saying Google BigQuery teams

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:17.670
with industry leaders.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.310
See how many friends and
followers this person had.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.041
And follow all the tweets
and the social media

00:02:23.041 --> 00:02:24.790
about the Google events
that are happening

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:26.040
over the next couple of weeks.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.690
So what we're
working against here

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:33.710
is a live BigQuery data set.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:36.730
Let me show you a little
bit more about that.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:39.510
If we click into Tableau Online
again, and look at the data

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:42.490
sources, you can see
that I've actually

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:46.440
been working against this data
source called tweets from Gnip,

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.120
and it's a live data source.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.450
And if I want to take
another closer look at it,

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:55.070
I can see that the
credentials for this data set

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:58.640
were actually embedded
right into the data source.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.450
Now, I can edit
that and ask users

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:03.690
to put their own credentials
in, but in this case,

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.282
we're going to go ahead and
keep the embedded credentials.

00:03:06.282 --> 00:03:07.740
So working with
Tableau Online, you

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.640
can actually publish BigQuery
as a live data source

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.194
or you can publish workbooks.

00:03:14.194 --> 00:03:16.110
And once you've got the
data source published,

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.810
you can even go in and
create a new workbook.

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:21.340
So let's do that against
this Twitter data set.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.810
And now you see a drag
and drop environment

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.990
that Tableau has that
lets you explore the data.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:33.320
On the left, you see
all the different fields

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:35.629
in this data set
about Twitter data.

00:03:35.629 --> 00:03:37.170
And what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.820
to go ahead and look at the
hashtags in this data set.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:41.970
I'm going to drag
that out to rows.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:45.410
And Tableau is doing a live
query against Google BigQuery

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:46.960
to try and look at
all the hashtags

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.010
that are in this data set.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:51.000
Now I want to see
how many tweets there

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:51.990
are for each of these.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.870
So I'm going to take number of
records, and in this data set,

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:57.404
every record is a tweet.

00:03:57.404 --> 00:03:58.820
I'm going to drag
that to columns.

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:04.570
Now I can sort down and see
what the biggest hashtag was

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:06.780
and bigdata is the
biggest hashtag.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.190
It is the one that
we were pulling on.

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:14.140
I can do a couple of fun things
like change the color here.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:25.432
And I can add things like the
actor display name to the view.

00:04:25.432 --> 00:04:26.890
And what this lets
me do is it lets

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:28.730
me break down each
one of these bars

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:31.645
into the different Twitterers--
the different people

00:04:31.645 --> 00:04:32.770
tweeting about the hashtag.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:40.660
So I can click and I can
see that NoSQL-- the account

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.810
NoSQL-- tweets a lot of times
with the hashtag #bigdata.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:45.160
That makes sense to me.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:46.940
I can tool tip over
another area and I

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:48.731
can see that there's
somebody else tweeting

00:04:48.731 --> 00:04:52.780
a lot about big data with the
display name egbertvanwinkley

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:55.830
and IAREGANG.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:58.330
So these are some accounts
you may even follow.

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.350
And we can also see
related hashtags here.

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:04.990
We've got cloud and health care
and a few other things as well.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:07.870
Now, one of the nice
things you can do here

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:12.270
is you can go ahead
and filter based

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:15.170
on when these tweets happen.

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:18.240
So I'm going to go ahead and put
posted time into a filter here.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:26.080
And I'm going to
filter again live right

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.655
in the browser against a live
data set of BigQuery tweets.

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:34.040
I'm going to filter to the last
few days-- the last two days,

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:34.650
in fact.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:37.610
06/08 to 06/10, and today
happens to be 06/10.

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:40.930
And now you can see, again,
the graph has changed.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:42.680
We've got less data
in there and we're

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:47.211
looking only at the tweets
in the last couple of days.

00:05:47.211 --> 00:05:49.460
Now, with Tableau you can
do a lot of different things

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:50.880
with your data.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.880
One of the things you
often find in data

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:56.840
is location information--
geographic information.

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:59.120
So I'm going to go
ahead and double click

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:00.320
on location country here.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:01.620
You see a little globe by it.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:04.260
And what that is telling me
is that Tableau has identified

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:07.060
country as a geographic field.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:09.560
And when I click on it, I
can see the location of all

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:12.642
the places where I have tweets.

00:06:12.642 --> 00:06:14.600
Now, I'm going to go back
to number of records,

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:17.360
and I'm going to go
ahead and double click

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:18.700
on that to add it to the view.

00:06:24.562 --> 00:06:28.294
And I'm going to go ahead
and crank up the size here.

00:06:28.294 --> 00:06:31.250
And you can see that the United
States has been the place where

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:33.620
I've had the most records.

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:35.829
But I want to show you
another neat thing you can do.

00:06:35.829 --> 00:06:37.495
When you're working
with data, you often

00:06:37.495 --> 00:06:38.760
want to change perspective.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:41.590
And Tableau's Show Me tool
lets you do that simply

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:44.240
by clicking in and choosing
another visualization that

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:45.115
might be appropriate.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:49.857
We also have a
handy back button.

00:06:49.857 --> 00:06:51.940
So if you want to undo
something, you can do that.

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:56.590
You can actually undo or redo
as many steps as you'd like.

00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:58.400
But I happen to like
this view right now,

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.880
so I'm going to go ahead and
save this as a new workbook.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:07.010
And I'm going to
call this tweets

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:14.350
on big data in this same
project and embed the password.

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:17.724
Tableau Online is a fully
secure analytics product,

00:07:17.724 --> 00:07:20.390
so you can make sure that you're
enforcing the security that you

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:23.210
have on your data.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:31.860
And now when I go back
into Tableau Online,

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:34.580
you can see that I've got
that new workbook here--

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:39.570
tweets on big data-- that was
updated just a few minutes ago.

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:41.100
That's just a taste
of what you can

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.910
do with Tableau Online
and Google BigQuery

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:46.760
when you establish a live
cloud-to-cloud connection

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:48.600
between this
powerful data source

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:52.180
and an analytics
product in the cloud.

